<header><p class="enc-kicker">State</p><h1 class="enc-title">Kingdom of Sine</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Sine</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Kingdom of Sine was a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serer">Serer</a> kingdom in present-day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/senegal">Senegal</a>, located in the region dominated by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jolof-empire">Jolof Empire</a>. It was among the polities associated with the Jolof confederation in its early period. Sine retained its own monarchy and identity over a long period and survived as a kingdom into the colonial era.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-sine.jpg" alt="Kingdom of Sine" width="320" height="219" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: HetmanTheResearcher · CC BY 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Kingdom_of_Sine_Map.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>Sine, or Siin in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serer">Serer</a>, lay along the north bank of the Saloum River delta. According to the historian Dennis Galvan, the Serer peoples migrated south from the Futa Tooro region from around the eleventh century, some of them fleeing King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/war-jabi">War Jabi</a> of Takrur, who imposed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sharia">Sharia</a> law and persecuted those who kept their traditional beliefs. Before the kingdom, some 60 villages were divided among states ruled by lamans, landowners and guardians of Serer spirituality, whose families, among them the Sarr, Joof and Ngom, formed a Great Council of Lamanes to settle disputes.</p><p>In the 13th or 14th century <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mandinka">Mandinka</a> migrants led by the matrilineal <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guelowar">Guelowar</a> clan arrived from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kaabu-empire">Kaabu</a> after losing a dynastic struggle there. The council granted them asylum, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serer">Serer</a> noblemen married Guelowar women, whose children ruled as Serer kings. Serer oral history holds that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maysa-wali">Maysa Wali</a>, after serving as legal adviser to the council, was chosen to rule and was crowned by Lamaan Jame Ngom of Faajaal; he reigned from about 1350 to 1370. The lamans became provincial chiefs but kept the power to depose a king.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Legend says that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maysa-wali">Maysa Wali</a> chose <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ndiadiane-ndiaye">Ndiadiane Ndiaye</a> as first Emperor of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jolof-empire">Jolof</a> about 1360. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serer">Serer</a> tradition holds that Sine never paid tribute, while the historian Sylviane Diouf writes that each <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassal</a> kingdom did. Sine and Saloum were the first to leave the Jolof Empire, at least twenty-nine years before the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-danki">Battle of Danki</a> in 1549. On 18 July 1867 King Kumba Ndoffene fa Maak Joof defeated the Muslim cleric Maba Diakhou Bâ at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-fandane-thiouthioune">Battle of Fandane-Thiouthioune</a>, where Maba was killed. The kings kept their title, Maad a Sinig, through the colonial period until the death of Mahecor Joof in 1969, and in 2019 the monarchy was revived as a ceremonial office.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>Sine lived from farming, chiefly <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/millet">millet</a>, and fishing. Rooted in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serer-religion">Serer religion</a>, its farmers long refused to grow <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/groundnut">groundnuts</a> for the French market, and the kingdom was less exposed to hunger and debt than its neighbours. Under the king stood the heirs apparent, the Buumi, Thilas and Loul; the Great Jaraaf, head of the noble council that elected kings from the royal family; the Great Farba Kaba, who commanded the army; and the Lingeer, the queen mother, who headed the female court.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Kingdom of Sine” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Ndiadiane Ndiaye is credited with drawing the Wolof states of Waalo, Cayor and Baol, together with the Serer kingdoms of <b>Sine</b> and Saloum, into a loose confederation centred on the inland province of Jolof.</blockquote><a href="/empire/jolof-empire">Jolof Empire · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The Raan festival is held at the old village of Tukar, founded by Lamane Jegan Joof around the 11th century after he migrated from Lambaye, north of <b>Sine</b>, guided by Saint Luguuñ Joof.</blockquote><a href="/religion/serer-religion">Serer religion · Origins</a></li></ul></section>
